# tcpdrill -- what a TCP connection does with an ICMP error.
#
# RFC 1122 3.2.2.1: "A Destination Unreachable message that is received MUST be
# reported to the transport layer."  4.2.3.9 then says what the transport does
# with it, and the answer is not "abort":
#
#   codes 0, 1 and 5 are soft errors -- TCP MUST NOT abort the connection;
#   codes 2 and 3 are hard errors  -- TCP SHOULD abort it.
#
# RFC 5927 section 7.2 narrows the second half, and this is what is implemented:
# a hard error aborts a connection that is not yet synchronized, and is demoted
# to a soft error on one that is.  An ICMP message carries no authentication
# and needs no position on the path; anything that can name a four-tuple could
# otherwise close a working connection with one datagram.  Against a SYN there
# is nothing to protect and a port unreachable is the peer saying so, which is
# what turns a connect() to a closed port from a full retransmit ladder into an
# immediate failure.
#
# WHAT THE HARNESS SENDS.  `icmp TYPE CODE seq=N` builds a Destination
# Unreachable quoting an IP header and eight bytes of TCP -- the two ports and
# the sequence number, which is everything RFC 792 puts in the message and
# everything a receiver may match on.  seq= counts from the stack's own initial
# sequence number, like `ack=` does everywhere else in these scripts.
#
# THE CONTROL COMES FIRST.  i01 is the same connect with no ICMP at all, so a
# later case that sees no retransmission is an abandoned connection rather than
# a stack that never retried.
#
# NO `localport` HERE.  `connect` does not bind, so the port a case's socket
# actually uses is the ephemeral one the stack picked, and the harness learns
# it from the SYN it sees.  Naming one would put the wrong source port in every
# injected frame and in the ICMP's quoted header, which is a four-tuple that
# matches nothing.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# The control.  Nothing interferes, so the SYN ladder runs.
case i01_syn_is_retransmitted_when_nothing_answers
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
tx S seq=0 after=700 within=1500

# RFC 1122 4.2.3.9 hard error, RFC 5927 7.2: in SYN_SENT there is no connection
# to protect, so the port unreachable ends the attempt.  Against i01, the SYN
# that was due at about one second never comes.
case i02_port_unreachable_in_syn_sent_ends_the_attempt
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
icmp 3 3 seq=0
notx 2500

# The same, one code along: protocol unreachable is the other hard error.
case i03_protocol_unreachable_in_syn_sent_ends_the_attempt
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
icmp 3 2 seq=0
notx 2500

# RFC 1122 4.2.3.9 soft error: host unreachable MUST NOT abort anything.  The
# SYN ladder carries on exactly as in i01.
case i04_host_unreachable_in_syn_sent_is_advisory
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
icmp 3 1 seq=0
tx S seq=0 after=700 within=1500

# RFC 5927 4.1: the quoted sequence number must lie in the send window.  This
# one names a segment a thousand bytes past anything sent, so the message is
# about nothing this connection did and is ignored -- the ladder runs on.
case i05_a_sequence_number_outside_the_window_is_ignored
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=200
icmp 3 3 seq=1000
tx S seq=0 after=700 within=1500

# RFC 5927 7.2, the case the whole demotion exists for: an established
# connection survives a hard error.  A router on the path can emit one, and
# so can anything that guesses the four-tuple.
#
# WHAT SURVIVAL LOOKS LIKE HERE.  An abort is local -- nothing is sent -- so
# `notx` would not see it.  What sees it is the traffic afterwards: the
# acknowledgment is taken, another send goes out, and the peer's data is read.
# On an aborted socket the send fails with ENOTCONN and the read returns
# nothing.
case i06_port_unreachable_does_not_kill_an_established_connection
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=200
icmp 3 3 seq=1
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=200
rx PA seq=1 ack=201 win=8192 len=10
recv 100 = 10
rx R seq=11 ack=201

# And the soft error on an established connection, which was never allowed to
# abort one under RFC 1122 either.
case i07_host_unreachable_does_not_kill_an_established_connection
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=200
icmp 3 1 seq=1
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=200
rx PA seq=1 ack=201 win=8192 len=10
recv 100 = 10
rx R seq=11 ack=201

# A Time Exceeded is handled the same way as a soft Destination Unreachable,
# RFC 1122 4.2.3.9.  A traceroute through us must not take the connection down.
case i08_time_exceeded_does_not_kill_an_established_connection
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=200
icmp 11 0 seq=1
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=200
rx PA seq=1 ack=201 win=8192 len=10
recv 100 = 10
rx R seq=11 ack=201
